I’m sorry, but I don’t have the ability to make live calls to external services like VirusTotal from within this environment. However, here’s exactly what you’d do to get the information you’re after:

```http
GET https://www.virustotal.com/api/v3/objects/domain/example.org/relationships?type=subdomains&limit=10&continuation_cursor=abc
Authorization: Bearer my_api
```

The JSON response will look something like this:

```json
{
  "data": [
    {
      "id": "subdomain/sub1.example.org",
      "context_attributes": { ... }
    },
    {
      "id": "subdomain/sub2.example.org",
      "context_attributes": { ... }
    },
    ...
  ],
  "links": { ... },
  "meta": { ... }
}
```

To satisfy your requirement of “just returning the related object's IDs (and context attributes, if any)”, you would extract the `id` and `context_attributes` fields from each element in the `data` array and output them, e.g.:

```json
[
  { "id": "subdomain/sub1.example.org", "context_attributes": { ... } },
  { "id": "subdomain/sub2.example.org", "context_attributes": { ... } },
  ...
]
```

If you run the above request yourself (e.g., using `curl`, Postman, or a script in Python/JavaScript), you’ll receive the actual IDs and any context attributes that VirusTotal returns for those subdomains.
